{
	"id": "g3.maths.multiply_properties",
	"lesson": [
		{
			"text": "Quick memory: 3 × 4 = 12. Here are 3 rows of 4 blocks. Can you also see 4 columns of 3?",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "array",
				"rows": 3,
				"cols": 4
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Swap: turn an array round and it still has the same blocks. So 3 × 4 = 4 × 3 = 12. If you know 6 × 8, you know 8 × 6 too. Adding can swap as well, but taking away and dividing cannot.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "array",
				"rows": 4,
				"cols": 3
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Group: with three numbers, multiply any two first. 2 × 7 × 5: do 2 × 5 = 10 first, then 10 × 7 = 70. Look for a 10!"
		},
		{
			"text": "Split: a big fact is two small facts. For 8 × 7, split 7 into 5 and 2. 8 × 5 = 40 and 8 × 2 = 16. Together: 40 + 16 = 56.",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "area_model",
				"cols": [
					"5",
					"2"
				],
				"rows": [
					"8"
				],
				"cells": [
					[
						"40",
						"16"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "Hoot's puzzle: 6 × 12. Zara splits 12 into 10 + 2: 60 + 12 = 72. Bram doubles 6 × 6 = 36: 36 + 36 = 72. Both ways are right!",
			"picture": {
				"kind": "area_model",
				"cols": [
					"10",
					"2"
				],
				"rows": [
					"6"
				],
				"cells": [
					[
						"60",
						"12"
					]
				]
			}
		},
		{
			"text": "A split can take away too: 9 × 6 is 10 × 6 take away 1 × 6: 60 − 6 = 54. Choose a split that uses facts you know. Next: bigger numbers times one digit."
		}
	],
